  "evidence": "A temporary offset is visible in N/E/U between indices 42 and 45: at index 42, N drops sharply from ~0.26 to -0.82, E rises from ~0.197 to -0.735, U drops from ~-0.549 to 1.853; then at index 45, N jumps back to -1.33 (still low), E drops to 0.545, U drops to -0.891 — but the full return is not immediate. However, a clearer temporary offset occurs between indices 61 and 64: at index 61, N = 0.24992, E = -0.82374, U = 1.29267; index 62: N = -0.40129, E = -1.41264, U = -0.50873 (abrupt shift); index 63: N = 0.20689, E = -0.70492, U = 0.30132 (partial recovery); index 64: N = -0.81912, E = 1.22201, U = -0.1657 — not symmetric. Re-examining: the only clean temporary offset with sustained shift and opposing return across all axes is between indices 102 and 105: index 102: N=1.28298, E=-1.11923, U=-0.02556; index 103: N=0.31652, E=0.52972, U=-2.43344 (large jump in all axes); index 104: N=-1.79982, E=0.5094, U=0.21452 (further shift); index 105: N=-0.91211, E=0.17802, U=-1.55759 — still not returning. After careful inspection of the full series, the only unambiguous temporary offset is between indices 42 and 44: at index 42 (N= -0.81775, E= -0.73457, U= -0.1083), index 43 (N= -1.60676, E= 0.28268, U= 0.19031) — abrupt multi-axis shift; then index 44 is missing (null); index 45 returns to N= -0.14335, E=0.78631, U= -1.28499 — not matching pre-offset level, so not a platform. Actually, the only interval satisfying *sustained shift over multiple samples followed by opposing return across same components* is indices 202–205: index 202: N=40.19439, E=24.11178, U=-8.01031; 203: N=41.66649, E=26.19324, U=-7.5283 (jump up in N/E, up in U); 204: N=41.16707, E=25.29987, U=-8.28423; 205: N=41.66342, E=23.94536, U=-7.74432 — no clear return. Given strict criteria and absence of any multi-sample sustained offset with clear opposing return across all three axes, and considering missing data breaks continuity, the correct conclusion is: no valid temporary offset intervals meet the definition.",
